# Per-machine symlinks, generated at startup — NEVER commit them (WI-39462).
#
# bin/docs-viewer.sh:84 does `ln -sfn "$path/docs" "$PROJECTS_CONTENT/$slug"` for
# every project in ~/.restart-harness-projects.json, a registry whose contents are
# specific to whoever is running the viewer. A symlink's git blob content IS its
# target string, so committing one commits an ABSOLUTE PATH from that machine —
# which is exactly what happened: `restart` and `sheets` shipped the owner's home
# path (/home/<owner>/...) inside every release's source.tar.zst for months.
#
# It stayed invisible because `grep -r` never reads a symlink and never descends
# into a symlinked directory, so no working-tree scan we own could see it. Only
# the tar linkname manifest could — and that was blind for .tar.zst until WI-39462.
#
# These links are regenerated on every `docs-viewer.sh` startup, so ignoring them
# costs nothing: the viewer still resolves them locally.
*
!.gitignore
